{"type": "thinking", "text": "Restored. So variables and totals work fine with edits.\n\nHmm, but there's still Undo disabled after all these edits. That's weird. Undo/Redo always disabled for files (not scratchpad). This might be a real regression: text edits in files don't enable Undo. But is it seeded? The task areas don't mention undo. But \"Reversal: undo...\" is explicitly suggested. Hmm. It could be a bug worth reporting. But maybe undo only applies to line add/delete/structural edits, and text edits are considered \"edits\" that should be undoable. The button was there earlier and in scratchpad worked, so likely it should work in files too. Hmm.\n\nActually wait: In scratchpad, after I typed \"a = 10\" (creating a line), Undo was ENABLED (element 45 [clickable]). That was a text edit plus perhaps line creation. In files, Undo stays disabled even after text edits. Could be because we are editing a *saved* file with existing history... no.\n\nHold on: Maybe Undo only works after the keyboard's \"Return\"/new line? In file, we only edited digits, not created a new line. In scratchpad, the Undo enabled after first line creation and after newline. Maybe undo history records line-level changes only, not in-place character edits. Hmm, but the undo after \"a = 10a * 2\" reverted the text content—it may have reverted the line insertion plus text.\n\nThis could be intentional: undo/redo in this app tracks line add/remove/edit submissions, and in-place edits might be committed only when you leave the field? We didn't leave the field. Let me test: after editing salary to 2500 and pressing BACK (dismiss keyboard) maybe Undo becomes enabled? Earlier in Percentages after BACK, Undo was still disabled. Hmm.\n\nWait, earlier in Percentages after finishing, I pressed BACK and Undo still disabled. So no.\n\nLet me test a line add operation in a file: press Enter to add a blank line, check Undo. That tells if undo is structural-only. But I don't want to alter files. Could use scratchpad (already shows undo works).\n\nGiven time, maybe focus on the named areas. But \"variables\" bug still not found. Let me test some specific variable edge cases:\n\n1. Reassignment and forward reference. Create in scratchpad:\n```\na = 10\nb = a + 1\na = 5\nc = a + b\n```\nExpected top-to-bottom: a=10, b=11, a=5, c=5+11=16. If the \"simpler pass\" wrongly uses the last assigned value for earlier lines, b would be 6. This is a classic bug with a map-based single pass evaluating all after collecting the final variables. The description \"assignments are now resolved from top to bottom by a simpler evaluation pass\" suggests they fixed the opposite. But maybe the seeded regression is that it does NOT respect top-to-bottom for re-assignments, i.e., b becomes 6. That would be a great test!\n\nWe can build this in the scratchpad using keyboard keys. It's slow but doable ~ 20 taps. Alternatively, use an existing file and add lines via typing with Gboard keys.\n\nActually, we can leverage the \"type_text\" if used with `clear:true` on a field? The type_text into a focused field replaced content weirdly. But maybe type_text works if we tap the field first ourselves and then call type_text without element (focus already). Earlier that resulted in concatenation/merging. Hmm.\n\nMaybe type_text uses ADB broadcast and the app receives chars; the newline keyboard issues cause scrambling. But typing a full string without spaces maybe works better? The first attempt typed \"b = a * 2\" and got \"b = a * 2a = 10\" because it inserted at start of line 1? Unclear.\n\nLet's use the on-screen keyboard taps but minimize characters. In scratchpad we can create lines with short names:\n```\na = 10\nb = a + 1\na = 5\nc = a + b\n```\nCharacters needed: line1 already exists (a = 10).\nLine2: b, space, =, a, space, +, space, 1 → keys: b, (space), =, a, space, +, space, 1. We can use app keypad for =, +, 1 and space via Gboard. ~8 taps.\nLine3: a, space, =, space, 5 → 5 taps.\nLine4: c, space, =, a, space, +, space, b → 8.\nTotal ~21 taps plus newline 4. That's a lot but feasible. But is it worth it? If the bug exists, great find. If not, costly.\n\nAlternative: Use the existing Remainders file which has `a = 50` at line 4 and `a % 14` at line 5 (currently Err due to %). We could add another line `total` after? Not helpful.\n\nWhat about editing `a = 50` in Remainders then check if line 5 uses new value — but line 5 is % which errors. Can't verify.\n\nExpenses: rent/food/fuel -> total/avg. Editing rent updates total & avg correctly? We tested price; but avg depends on total/3 maybe. Let's test editing rent in Expenses to 100 and check total & avg. This tests variables + dynamic var `total`/`avg`. But likely works.\n\nHmm. The re-assignment test is the strongest candidate for a seeded \"top to bottom\" bug. Let's do it. But typing is painful. Maybe we can use a different trick: use the \"global\" file which might already have variables? Let's check global file contents first - maybe it has a chain we can leverage without typing. Tap home then \"Open global file\" [22]. But we're in Monthly budget with keyboard. Press BACK then home? Let's press BACK to dismiss keyboard, then Back to home.\n\nActually, we can stay: press BACK twice? The first BACK dismisses keyboard, second goes home. Let's do BACK (keyboard) then tap Back.\n\nActually, there's a thought: maybe the seeded variables bug is simpler — in a *newly added* line the assignment isn't recognized until re-edit, or references to a variable defined on a previous line fail. We saw scratchpad \"b =2\" worked. Let's test a reference in scratchpad quickly: line2 already \"b =2\". Change it to \"b = a * 2\" would need letters. Hmm.\n\nLet's check the global file - maybe it has example variables including a chain. Tap home Back then global file.\n\nLet me press BACK to dismiss keyboard."}
